WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
The lethal trifecta, applied to an agent.

2
00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:05.976
You met Willison’s lethal trifecta in Studio 1.

3
00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:16.304
An agent that has all three of these can be tricked into stealing data: access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, and a way to communicate externally.

4
00:00:16.584 --> 00:00:22.173
The attacker uses the second to instruct the agent to read the first and send it through the third.

5
00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:26.272
Agents tend to collect all three without anyone deciding to.

6
00:00:26.552 --> 00:00:37.517
A status-report agent reads issues (untrusted), reads internal notes or account data to judge priority (private), and posts updates or fetches links (outbound).

7
00:00:37.797 --> 00:00:39.803
Each tool was added for a good reason.

8
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:41.811
Together they are the trifecta.

9
00:00:42.091 --> 00:00:45.525
The practical value of the framing is that it tells you where to cut.

10
00:00:45.805 --> 00:00:52.355
You don’t have to make the model unfoolable; you have to make sure that, at any moment, the three are not all live at once.

11
00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:55.707
Options, from strongest to weakest: Remove a leg.

12
00:00:55.987 --> 00:01:01.299
Drop the outbound tools, or keep private data out of this agent and give it to a different one.

13
00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:03.904
Separate legs in time or in components.

14
00:01:04.184 --> 00:01:17.987
Read untrusted content in a step that has no private data and no outbound tools, and pass forward only constrained results (a list of issue numbers, a structured summary), never free text that could carry instructions.

15
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.760
Gate the leg that remains.

16
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:29.427
Once untrusted content and private data have both entered the context, every outbound action needs a deterministic check, and possibly a human.

17
00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:31.648
Watch the subtle outbound channels.

18
00:01:31.928 --> 00:01:38.051
A URL in a fetch is outbound, because the query string carries data to whoever runs that server.

19
00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:53.264
A Markdown image in a chat message is outbound the moment a client renders it, because rendering requests the image URL; Google lists Markdown sanitization and suspicious-URL redaction among its defenses for this reason.

20
00:01:53.544 --> 00:01:59.560
A link the user clicks, an email draft, a PR description: all of them can carry data out.
